the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a set* is a subset of *a set*,
    *a thing* is a set,
    *a thing* belongs to *a set*.

the knowledge base subset includes:

a set A is a subset of a set B
    if A is a set
    and B is a set
    and for all cases in which  
        a thing belongs to A
        it is the case that
        the thing belongs to B.

scenario lists is:
    [Alice, Bob] is a set.
    [Alice] is a set.

    a thing belongs to a set
        if the thing is in the set.
    % Only [Alice Bob] and [Alice] are sets here, so those are the only candidates:
    % [Alice Bob] is not a subset of [Alice] (Bob is missing), and [Bob] is not a
    % set at all. A list value renders space-separated inside its brackets.
    subset expects answers ["[Alice Bob] is a subset of [Alice Bob]", "[Alice] is a subset of [Alice Bob]", "[Alice] is a subset of [Alice]"].

scenario facts is:
    family one is a set.
    family two is a set.
   
    Bob belongs to family one.
    Alice belongs to family one.
   
    Alice belongs to family two.
    % family one = {Bob, Alice}, family two = {Alice}: so two is a subset of one,
    % NOT the other way round — Bob belongs to one and not to two.
    subset expects answers ["family one is a subset of family one", "family two is a subset of family one", "family two is a subset of family two"].
   
query subset is:
    which set is a subset of which other set.